ng both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound** – Show that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   must contain at least two non‑sunny lines.  \n   * The diagonal \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   * A sunny line can contain **at most one** of these points, because two points with the same sum force slope \\(-1\\).  \n   * Hence, to cover all points of \\(D\\) we need either the whole line \\(D\\) (a non‑sunny line) or at least \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   * If we use only the line \\(D\\) as the single non‑sunny line, the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) remain uncovered.  \n     A sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can contain at most one of them; therefore a second non‑sunny line (e.g. \\(x=1\\)) is necessary.  \n   * Consequently at least two lines are non‑sunny, i.e.  \n\n     \\[\n     k\\le n-2 .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Fix \\(k\\).*\n\n   * **Two fixed non‑sunny lines**  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{v}\\colon x=1 ,\\qquad L_{d}\\colon x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     \\(L_{v}\\) covers all points with abscissa \\(1\\); \\(L_{d}\\) covers the whole diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n   * **Remaining points**  \n\n     \\[\n     S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     Geometrically \\(S\\) is the integer right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((2,2),(n-2,2),(2,n-2)\\).\n\n   * **\\(k\\) sunny lines** – for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i\\colon\\; \\text{the line through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(i,n-i).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     Its equation is  \n\n     \\[\n     y-n =\\frac{-(i)}{\\,i-1\\,}(x-1),\\qquad\\text{slope }m_i=-\\frac{i}{i-1}.\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(i\\ge2\\), \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n     The point \\((i,n-i)\\in S\\) lies on \\(S_i\\); thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n     \\[\n     (2,n-2),\\;(3,n-3),\\dots ,(k+1,n-(k+1)).\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n   * **\\(n-2-k\\) additional non‑sunny lines** – for every integer \\(b\\) with  \n\n     \\[\n     2\\le b\\le n-2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\notin\\{n-2,n-3,\\dots ,n-(k+1)\\},\n     \\]\n\n     take the horizontal line  \n\n     \\[\n     H_b\\colon y=b .\n     \\]\n\n     Each \\(H_b\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and contains **all** points of \\(S\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).\n\n   * **Counting the lines**  \n\n     \\[\n     \\underbrace{1}_{L_v}+ \\underbrace{1}_{L_d}\n     +\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_i}\n     +\\underbrace{(n-2-k)}_{\\text{horizontal }H_b}\n     = n .\n     \\]\n\n     All \\(n\\) lines are distinct: the two base lines have slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); the sunny lines have distinct slopes \\(-\\frac{i}{i-1}\\); the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\).\n\n   * **Coverage** – every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to one of the following groups  \n\n     * \\(x=1\\) → \\(L_v\\);  \n     * \\(x+y=n+1\\) → \\(L_d\\);  \n     * points of \\(S\\) with \\(y=b\\) where \\(b\\) is among the chosen horizontal levels → the corresponding \\(H_b\\);  \n     * the \\(k\\) points listed in (4) → the sunny line \\(S_i\\) that contains them.\n\n     Hence all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   * Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n3. **Conclusion** – the upper bound (1) and the explicit construction together give the complete set of possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D\\) contains exactly the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D\\).\n\n*Proof.* If a line contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\) of \\(D\\), then  \n\\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\). Hence \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\) and the slope equals \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nConsequently, to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\) we need either the whole line \\(D\\) (which is non‑sunny) or at least \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one is non‑sunny.  \nIf that non‑sunny line is not \\(D\\), then the points of (1) must be covered by sunny lines, which is impossible because at most one point of (1) can lie on each sunny line.  \nHence the unique non‑sunny line must be \\(D\\).\n\nNow the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) are still uncovered.  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can contain at most one of these points.  \nSince there are \\(n\\) such points and we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines left, at least one more non‑sunny line is required.\n\nThus **any** admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non‑sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Two fixed non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}\\colon x=1 ,\\qquad   \nL_{d}\\colon x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{v}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with abscissa \\(1\\);  \n\\(L_{d}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S\\) is the integer right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((2,2),(n-2,2),(2,n-2)\\).\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(i,n-i). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-n = -\\frac{i}{\\,i-1\\,}(x-1),\\qquad\\text{slope }m_i=-\\frac{i}{i-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\nm_i\\neq0,\\qquad m_i\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m_i\\neq-1,\n\\]\n\nso each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((i,n-i)\\in S\\) lies on \\(S_i\\); the \\(k\\) lines therefore cover the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n\\[\n(2,n-2),\\;(3,n-3),\\dots ,(k+1,n-(k+1)). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4  Remaining non‑sunny lines  \n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus\\{n-2,n-3,\\dots ,n-(k+1)\\},\n\\]\n\ntake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b\\colon y=b . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_b\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and contains **all** points of \\(S\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).\n\nThe number of such horizontal lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)-k = n-2-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5  Counting the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1}_{L_v}+ \\underbrace{1}_{L_d}\n+ \\underbrace{k}_{S_i}\n+ \\underbrace{(n-2-k)}_{H_b}\n= n .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct:\n* \\(L_v\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\)