  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{t}\\colon y=x+t\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(1\\) (hence it is sunny) and meets \\(S\\) in the points\n   \\(\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n-t-2\\}\\).\n\n   – For the same integer \\(t\\) the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{t}\\colon y=2+t\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   is non‑sunny and meets \\(S\\) in the points\n   \\(\\{(a,2+t)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n-t-2\\}\\).\n\n   Observe that for a fixed \\(t\\) the two sets (2) and (3) are identical\n   except that the former contains the point \\((2,2+t)\\) while the latter\n   contains all the other points of the same row.  Consequently, **if we keep\n   the sunny line \\(M_{t}\\) we may discard the horizontal line \\(H_{t}\\); if we\n   discard \\(M_{t}\\) we must keep \\(H_{t}\\) in order to cover the row\n   \\(y=2+t\\).**\n\n   *Choosing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any subset \\(\\mathcal T\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-4\\}\\) with\n   \\(|\\mathcal T|=k\\).  \n   - For every \\(t\\in\\mathcal T\\) keep the sunny line \\(M_{t}\\).  \n   - For every \\(t\\notin\\mathcal T\\) keep the horizontal line \\(H_{t}\\).\n\n   The family of lines therefore consists of  \n\n   * the three base non‑sunny lines \\(L_{v},L_{h},L_{d}\\);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{M_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal T\\}\\);\n   * the \\((n-3)-k\\) horizontal lines \\(\\{H_{t}\\mid t\\notin\\mathcal T\\}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\;+\\;k\\;+\\;(n-3-k)=n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct because their slopes are respectively\n   \\(\\infty,0,-1,1\\) (the sunny lines) and \\(0\\) (the horizontals).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n   - Points with \\(x=1\\) lie on \\(L_{v}\\); points with \\(y=1\\) lie on \\(L_{h}\\);\n     points with \\(x+y=n+1\\) lie on \\(L_{d}\\).\n   - Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  Write \\(t=b-a\\).  Since \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and\n     \\(a+b\\le n\\), we have \\(0\\le t\\le n-4\\).  \n     If \\(t\\in\\mathcal T\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the s